  "documentation": {
    "range": "<p>This species is very widely distributed throughout all of the oceans, particularly in temperate waters, and some individuals have been found in more tropical waters (Jereb <em>et al.</em> 2014). Type locality: Presumed western Mediterranean Sea (Jereb&#160;<em>et al.</em>&#160;2014).</p>",
    "population": "There is no information on the population status of this species.  However, the estimated fisheries landing for this species was approximately 149,000 mt in the eastern-central Atlantic (ECA) in 2001, with most of this catch taken  in western Sahara and Mauritania waters. There is clear variation in landings between years, and recruitment, in Mauritanian waters at least, appears to be dependent on strong upwelling conditions.",
    "habitats": "This is a benthic species found in temperate, subtropical and tropical waters in temperatures ranging from 6 to 33 degrees Celsius.  It is stenohaline and is not found where salinity is less than 29 ppt.  It is known to undertake limited seasonal migrations.  Its diet is composed mainly of fish, crustaceans and other molluscs, but it has also been known to take polychaete worms and ophiuroids.  It is preyed upon by large fish such as conger and moray eels and marine mammals such as the common and bottlenose dolphins.  However, the greatest mortality occurs during the planktonic phase (Jereb <em>et al. </em>2014).",
    "threats": "The major threat to this species is unregulated fishing.  It is taken by hand-jig, in pots, trammel nets and traps, but more significantly, it is the target species in bottom trawl fisheries off the Saharan Bank.  The estimated landing for this species in 2001 was 149,000 mt in the eastern-central Atlantic, most of which came from the Saharan Bank and Mauritanian waters.  However, given the wide distribution of this species, intensely targeted fishing in particular locations is unlikely to have a detrimental effect across the species range as a whole.",
    "measures": "<p>There are no conservation measures in place for this species. Research is required to determine whether harvesting has any significant impact and monitoring is essential.</p>",
    "use_trade": "<p>This species&#160;is often considered as the most important fished cephalopod species in the Mediterranean and is highly valuable economically. Northwest Africa is the largest fishery in the world that fishes just one single species (Jereb <em>et al.</em> 2014). In order for the cultivation of this species to be considered there needs to be more research into the influence of environmental parameters and the identification of optimum conditions for cultivation in large tanks. More research can also help the trials for paralarval cultivation (Iglesias <em>et al.</em> 2014).</p>",
    "rationale": "<em>Octopus vulgaris</em> has been assessed as Least Concern because this is a widespread species extending throughout most of the Atlantic and some of the Pacific.  Although it is fished over much of its range and although this fishing is mostly unregulated, it is only subject to heavy fishing pressure in selected locations.",
    "trend_justification": null,
    "taxonomic_notes": "<p><strong> </strong>The genus <em>Octopus </em>is currently under taxonomic revision. It is for this reason that the species originally treated under the genus name <em>Octopus</em><strong> </strong>have provisionally remained as such until they are reviewed.</p>"
  },
